  "solution": "Step 1: Calculate the synchronous speed (<code>N_s</code>) of the motor. The synchronous speed is the speed of the rotating magnetic field and is determined by the frequency of the power supply and the number of poles in the motor.\nFormula: <code>N_s = (120 * f) / P</code>\nGiven: Frequency (<code>f</code>) = 50 Hz, Number of poles (<code>P</code>) = 4.\n<code>N_s = (120 * 50) / 4 = 6000 / 4 = 1500 rpm</code>.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the initial slip (<code>s₁</code>) at the speed <code>N₁ = 980 rpm</code>. Slip is the difference between synchronous speed and rotor speed, expressed as a fraction of synchronous speed.\nFormula: <code>s₁ = (N_s - N₁) / N_s</code>\n<code>s₁ = (1500 - 980) / 1500 = 520 / 1500</code>.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the final slip (<code>s₂</code>) at the desired speed <code>N₂ = 820 rpm</code>.\nFormula: <code>s₂ = (N_s - N₂) / N_s</code>\n<code>s₂ = (1500 - 820) / 1500 = 680 / 1500</code>.\n\nStep 4: Apply the condition for constant torque. For an induction motor under constant load torque, a key relationship derived from the torque equation is that the ratio of the total rotor resistance to the slip remains constant.\nCondition: <code>T₁ = T₂</code>\nThis leads to the relation: <code>R_total_1 / s₁ = R_total_2 / s₂</code>\nHere, <code>R_total_1 = R₂ = 0.95 Ω/ph</code> (initial rotor resistance).\nAnd <code>R_total_2 = R₂ + R_ext</code>, where <code>R_ext</code> is the external resistance to be added.\nSo, <code>R₂ / s₁ = (R₂ + R_ext) / s₂</code>.\n\nStep 5: Solve for the external resistance (<code>R_ext</code>).\nRearranging the formula from Step 4:\n<code>R₂ + R_ext = R₂ * (s₂ / s₁)</code>\n<code>R_ext = R₂ * (s₂ / s₁) - R₂</code>\n<code>R_ext = R₂ * ( (s₂ / s₁) - 1 )</code>\nSubstituting the values of slips and <code>R₂</code>:\n<code>s₂ / s₁ = (680/1500) / (520/1500) = 680 / 520 = 17 / 13</code>\n<code>R_ext = 0.95 * ( (17 / 13) - 1 )</code>\n<code>R_ext = 0.95 * ( (17 - 13) / 13 )</code>\n<code>R_ext = 0.95 * (4 / 13)</code>\n<code>R_ext = 3.8 / 13 ≈ 0.2923 Ω/ph</code>.",
